# Called directly by an active engine node against the UI bridge's
# ~/request_user_input server, bypassing the Orchestrator entirely -- it
# has no visibility into this exchange. Lets an engine ask the user a
# question mid-task: a button choice (the engine supplies the panel
# content itself), an object tap on its engine's own overlay camera stream
# (rammp-UI-bridge docs/NODE_INTERFACE.md §3.3.1), or both at once.
#
# Only one engine may have a goal active against this action at a time,
# mirroring the Orchestrator's own one-engine-executing-at-a-time
# invariant; the bridge rejects a second concurrent goal. `token` must
# match the one most recently granted by the bridge's own
# ~/ui_control/acquire_control (the same arbitration resource that gates
# EngineOverlayImage frames, see rammp_common_interfaces' AcquireControl) --
# a goal with a stale/mismatched token is rejected outright.
#
# Tapping an object does NOT complete this goal by itself -- the user may
# tap several different objects in a row while deciding, and the engine
# may react to each one (e.g. update its own highlight overlay, or call
# ~/request_user_input/update to change the displayed message/candidate
# list/highlighted selection) without the goal ending. Each tap is
# reported as feedback instead. The goal only completes when the user
# presses a button (status "button") or the engine cancels (status
# "cancelled") -- by that point the engine is expected to already know
# (from the feedback stream) which object, if any, is selected, and the
# pressed button_id tells it what to do with that selection.
#
# `panel_json`/`objects`/`selected_instance_id` were previously three
# separate typed fields (one of them a ROS message array,
# `ObjectCandidate[]`). They are now folded into one `request_json` JSON
# string (see rammp-UI-bridge docs/INTERACTION_PANEL_SCHEMA.md) so every
# field on this goal lives in one place, mirroring how `panel_json` itself
# is already a JSON string. `ObjectCandidate.msg` is retired along with
# this (no longer referenced by any interface).

string engine_id          # the calling engine's own id (its CapabilityManifest.engine_id) --
                          # lets the bridge tag any pushed `objects` with this engine's own
                          # overlay camera stream_id (§3.3.1), and label log/error messages
uint8[16] token           # must match the currently-granted ~/ui_control token (see above)
string request_json
# JSON object:
#   {
#     "panel_json": str,          # optional, default "" -- InteractionPanel-shaped JSON
#                                 # (title/message/buttons/progress/style, see
#                                 # rammp-UI-bridge docs/INTERACTION_PANEL_SCHEMA.md) the
#                                 # engine wants shown; omitted/"" if this request is
#                                 # object-only
#     "objects": [                # optional, default [] -- candidate objects the user can
#                                 # tap on this engine's own overlay stream; omitted/[] if
#                                 # this request is panel-only
#       {"instance_id": str, "perception_class": str, "box": [x_min, y_min, x_max, y_max]}
#       # box is in the calling engine's own camera's pixel space
#     ],
#     "selected_instance_id": str  # optional, default "" -- one of objects' instance_id to
#                                 # show as already highlighted/selected when the goal
#                                 # starts; omitted/"" for no initial selection
#   }
# At least one of panel_json / objects must be non-empty.
---
string result_json
# JSON object:
#   {"status": "button", "button_id": str}   # user pressed one of panel_json's buttons
#   {"status": "cancelled"}                  # engine cancelled the goal before that happened
---
string feedback_json
# Sent once per object tap while the goal is still active (never after
# the result above is sent). Informational only -- the engine decides
# what, if anything, to do about it (e.g. call ~/request_user_input/update
# to reflect the new selection back to the user).
# JSON object:
#   {"event_type": "object_tapped", "instance_id": str}
